    "en":  {
               "headline":  "New 52-week low in a chip selloff, despite a quarter that beat and raised",
               "technical":  "NVTS closed at $6.08 on 20 Nov 2026. It fell 15.2% that day and finished just above its new 52-week low of $6.03, 82% below the 52-week high of $34.17. Trend: price is far below all three EMAs (exponential moving averages, which smooth out the price trend): EMA50 $9.69, EMA100 $11.17 and EMA200 $11.71. The stacking 50 \u003c 100 \u003c 200 is a classic downtrend, and price is about 37% below the EMA50. Momentum: MACD (a trend-momentum gauge) is at -0.72, below its signal line at -0.53, and the histogram is negative at -0.19. That means selling pressure is still growing, not fading. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) is 31.82, near oversold but not there yet. Volatility: price closed below the lower Bollinger Band at $6.56 (the bands are a 20-day average ± 2 standard deviations). That marks a stretched, extreme move. Such closes often lead to short bounces, but strong downtrends can keep going. Volume of 18.2M shares was about 1.26× the 20-day average of 14.4M, so the selling was real. Nearest support is the $6.03 low. The first resistance is near $6.56, then $8.53 (the band\u0027s middle line).",
               "fundamental":  "The business is improving even as the share price collapses. Q3 2026 revenue (reported 3 Nov) was $14.0M, up 33% from Q2 and 38% from a year earlier, at the top of guidance. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 40.0%. Q4 guidance is $16.0M ±$0.5M, about 14% growth over Q3. Navitas has mostly exited low-end mobile chips. It expects AI data-center and grid/energy products to pass 40% of sales by year-end, and AI revenue to roughly triple in 2027. On 6 Oct it closed the roughly $126M purchase of Claros (vertical power delivery). It also pointed to an 800V-to-12V board built with Nvidia and an 800V reference design with Microchip. Scale is still tiny: the Q3 GAAP net loss was $37.1M, and Q4 non-GAAP opex of about $25M is more than revenue. Cash of $531.5M and no debt give a long runway.",
               "news":  [
                            "3 Nov 2026: Q3 revenue of $14.0M (+38% year on year) beat the roughly $13.5M consensus, and Q4 was guided to $16.0M ±$0.5M. Management cited a 1-million-unit GaN order for an 800V power brick and a book-to-bill near 2.0 (orders about twice shipments). Shares rose 7.9% to $8.56 that day.",
                            "4 Nov 2026: Rosenblatt upgraded NVTS from Neutral to Buy with a $13 target. Separately, Needham reportedly kept its Buy rating but cut its target from $21 to $15.",
                            "12 Nov 2026: Navitas filed a second patent suit against Renesas in East Texas (six counts), aimed at Renesas\u0027s newly launched 650V SuperGaN part. It adds to an August suit, while trade-secret and patent claims that Renesas and Wolfspeed filed against Navitas in July are still pending.",
                            "12 Nov 2026: Nvidia announced that CEO Jensen Huang had resigned, with CFO Colette Kress as interim CEO. Nvidia is Navitas\u0027s flagship 800V partner, so AI-power stocks were hit; NVTS fell about 12% to $7.88 on 16 Nov.",
                            "20 Nov 2026: NVTS fell 15.2% to $6.08, a new 52-week low, during a broad chip selloff. I found no negative news specific to Navitas. Management is due to speak at a UBS investor conference on 1 Dec."
                        ],
               "caution":  "The chart is in a confirmed downtrend with no sign of turning. Price is below every EMA and the MACD gap is still widening. A close below the lower band at a fresh 52-week low can mark capitulation (a final wave of panic selling), but nothing confirms that yet. On fundamentals, about $16M of quarterly revenue against roughly $25M of opex means losses will continue. The stock\u0027s beta near 4 also means it tends to move about four times as much as the market. Patent fights with Renesas and Wolfspeed carry legal costs and uncertain outcomes, and Nvidia\u0027s leadership change could slow its decisions on the 800V roadmap.",
               "opportunity":  "The selloff appears driven by sector sentiment (Nvidia\u0027s CEO exit and a chip-wide selloff), not by Navitas\u0027s own results, which beat estimates and guided higher. RSI near 31 and a close below the lower Bollinger Band show the stock is stretched. Readings like these often come before relief bounces, though the timing is unpredictable. For long-term holders, the key points to check at the 1 Dec UBS conference are: $531.5M of cash (roughly a third of the market value), book-to-bill near 2.0, the 1-million-unit 800V GaN order, and the goal of tripling AI revenue in 2027.",
               "risks":  [
                             "Nvidia\u0027s leadership uncertainty could delay or reshape the 800V data-center power roadmap that Navitas is betting on.",
                             "Pending patent and trade-secret lawsuits involving Renesas and Wolfspeed could bring costs or limits on products.",
                             "Losses continue: Q4 opex of about $25M against about $16M of revenue could eventually require raising money by issuing new shares.",
                             "Worries about slower AI data-center spending (higher borrowing costs, permit delays) hit high-beta suppliers hardest.",
                             "Larger rivals such as Renesas are launching competing 650V GaN products despite the lawsuits."
                         ],
               "summary":  "Technically, NVTS is bearish. At $6.08 it sits at a 52-week low, below all three EMAs and the lower Bollinger Band, with MACD negative and RSI at 31.82, near oversold. The slide from $10.07 on 11 Nov came from shocks across the sector, mainly Nvidia\u0027s CEO resignation and a chip selloff, not from Navitas\u0027s own numbers, which beat and raised guidance. The GaN/SiC AI-power thesis is still early and unproven at scale. I mark it \u0027watch\u0027 because of uncertainty over Nvidia\u0027s roadmap, ongoing litigation and continued heavy losses."
